\description{
Four modes:
\itemize{
\item \strong{In-sample}: \code{predict(fit)} returns fitted values.
\item \strong{State posterior / NA-response}: \code{predict(fit, type = "state")} returns
the marginalised per-site occupancy posterior of a \code{"nested_laplace"} fit:
the per-row eta posterior is a Gaussian mixture over the hyperparameter
grid (per-cell fitted linear predictor and predictive variance), so \code{psi}
is its Gauss-Hermite mean and \code{psi_lower} / \code{psi_upper} are the mixture-CDF
quantiles -- a calibrated 95\% credible interval, exact for every latent
prior. Rows with \code{heldout = TRUE} are the INLA-style NA-response
prediction targets (single-season sites whose detection history was
all-missing), where occupancy is interpolated from the latent field rather
than informed by detections.
\item \strong{Design-matrix}: \code{predict(fit, X.0 = ...)} predicts at new covariate values.
\item \strong{Terms-based}: \code{predict(fit, terms = "elev")} varies one covariate, others at mean.
\item \strong{Joint occu_cover}: for a \code{occu_cover} joint-coupled fit,
\code{predict(fit, newdata, type = "occurrence" | "cover_cond" | "cover_exp" | "change")} samples the joint latent from the grid-integrated posterior
(the outer-grid mixture via \code{\link[tulpa:tulpa_posterior_draws]{tulpa::tulpa_posterior_draws()}}) and
marginalises every derived quantity per draw. \code{type = "change"} with
\code{times = c(t1, ..., tK)} (K >= 2) returns a per-cell change table. Each
quantity -- occupancy \code{psi}, conditional cover \code{cover_cond}, expected
cover \code{cover_exp} -- has a level column per step (\code{psi_T1..psi_TK}, ...)
carrying the posterior mean with \code{.sd} / \code{.lwr} / \code{.upr} at \code{level}, and a
delta per step against \code{t1} (\code{delta_psi}, \code{delta_cover_cond},
\code{delta_cover_exp}) with \code{.lwr} / \code{.upr} and \code{.prob_pos}, the posterior
probability \code{P(delta > 0)} per cell. The expected-cover change is split
exactly into its occupancy- and abundance-driven parts
(\code{delta_cover_from_occ}, \code{delta_cover_from_ab}), reported the same way.
With two times the delta columns are unsuffixed; with K > 2 each carries
\verb{_T<k>}. Every step is evaluated on ONE draw set, so levels and deltas
share a posterior and are jointly valid. A standalone \code{occu()} joint fit
returns the same layout with occupancy only. The result is a
\code{tobs_prediction} table (one row per cell) carrying per-unit \verb{[cell x nsim]}
draw matrices in \code{attr(, "draws")}; map it yourself, e.g.
\code{left_join(cents, pr, by = "cell")} then
\code{geom_tile(aes(x, y, fill = delta_psi))} (or \code{geom_sf()} on polygon cells).
\code{type = "trajectory"} with \code{times = c(t1, ..., tK)} predicts occupancy
\code{psi}, conditional cover \code{cover_cond} and expected cover \code{cover_exp} at
each time, for any times the user chooses, as a long table with the
posterior mean, median and \code{level} interval of each (one row per cell and
time); \code{aggregate = TRUE} averages over the cells per draw and gives the
overall trajectory, one row per time. All times share one draw set.
\item \strong{Community families}: \code{ms_occu()}, \code{ms_dyn_occu()}, \code{ms_int_occu()},
\code{ms_abun()}, \code{ms_distance()} and \code{ms_occu_cover()} predict per-species
matrices \verb{[rows x species]}, in-sample from \code{fitted()} and at \code{newdata}
from the per-species coefficients. Each family answers to its own response
types: \code{"occupancy"} / \code{"detection"} (\code{ms_occu}, \code{ms_dyn_occu},
\code{ms_int_occu}), \code{"abundance"} / \code{"detection"} (\code{ms_abun}),
\code{"lambda"} / \code{"sigma"} (\code{ms_distance}), and \code{"occupancy"} /
\code{"detection"} / \code{"cover_cond"} / \code{"cover_exp"} (\code{ms_occu_cover}). A
fit carrying an areal field or a latent-factor loading is in-sample only,
the field having no value at an unseen site.
\item \strong{Spatial-factor community}: for a reduced-rank spatial-factor
\code{ms_occu_cover()} fit, \code{predict(fit, type = "occupancy" | "cover_cond" | "cover_exp")} returns the per-species per-cell posterior as a long table
(one row per cell x species): \code{"occupancy"} gives \code{psi}, \code{"cover_cond"} the
conditional cover mean \code{E[cover | present]}, \code{"cover_exp"} the unconditional
expected cover \code{psi * E[cover | present]}, each with a \verb{_median} and a
\verb{_lower} / \verb{_upper} interval. The maps are marginalised over the loading +
field posterior, so a rare species borrows strength across the shared
factors for a calibrated map. The latent fields are tied to the cell graph,
so \code{X.0} / \code{newdata} prediction is not supported.
}
